Subject: [PATCH 0/4] sparc64: fix DMA cleanup, PROM output and IRQ validation

These four fixes address existing sparc64 error paths found while
preparing and reviewing Fujitsu M3000 support:

1. Release the IOMMU context when scatterlist mapping fails.
2. Advance PROM output by the bytes written and stop on no progress.
3. Stop cookie IRQ setup when allocation fails.
4. Check sun4u interrupt-vector bounds before indexing the table.

They are based on v7.3-rc1 and require no M3000 support. They also apply
to the SPARC for-next snapshot a9e88d617a25 and compose with the separate
ten-patch M3000 v4 series in either order. Patch 2 also affects the
M3000 early console if firmware returns short writes.

The PROM patch changes the old negative-return policy explicitly:
negative returns were retried indefinitely; now they terminate the
write along with zero progress or an impossible count. This can drop
remaining text on a transient error. It bounds software retrying, not
the duration of an individual firmware call.

Testing
-------
Review builds of this exact four-patch order passed full vmlinux at all
four commits with generic-SMP, including the sun4v cookie-IRQ path,
using GCC 13.3, binutils 2.42 and W=1. Existing FusionMPT/input warnings
remain; these are not warning-free builds.

The standalone tip passed a GCC 13.3 W=1 SMP vmlinux build and UP builds
of all affected objects. Existing FusionMPT warnings remain in the full
build. Targeted before/after harnesses cover context cleanup on forced
allocation failure, PROM short writes and return values, cookie IRQ
allocation failure and interrupt-vector boundaries.

The same code changes were present in the preceding combined M3000
candidate, which booted on M3000 SMP, Ultra45 UP and a T5120 SMP LDOM.
Each passed bounded COW, remap/protection and disk/network checksum
checks with taint zero. These are regression tests of the combined
candidate, not a boot test of this standalone series or hardware
injection of its failure cases. Oberon uses context zero, and the LDOM
has neither PCI passthrough nor cookie-only IRQ failure coverage.

Codex assisted with debugging, test instrumentation, code drafting,
review and commit-message preparation.

Magnus Lindholm (4):
